---
# The skill's invocation name, one word, lowercase — this is what `flow:commit`
# and `/commit` name in a harness.
name: {SKILL_NAME}

# Two lines that decide who finds this skill: the long text is what the
# model matches an invocation against, so say what it is for, not what it is.
description: {SKILL_DESCRIPTION}

# Who may invoke this skill (ADR-030). `model: true` lets the model pick it
# up on its own when the situation matches; `user: true` also offers it to
# the person as an explicit action. Any 2x2 combination is valid:
#   model: true,  user: true  — normal, discoverable (the default)
#   model: true,  user: false — background knowledge, never a command
#   model: false, user: true  — an explicit action only the person triggers
#   model: false, user: false — unused
invoke:
  model: true
  user: true

# Everything below this line is the skill's body, read only when it runs.
# Instructions here weigh against the work the skill is asked for.
---
Replace this paragraph with what the skill actually does, in the order a
reader needs it: the situation it answers, the steps it performs, and what
it must never do. Keep it short — it is loaded when the skill is invoked,
not before.
